---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleOffer from '../../components/ArticleOffer.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';

const title = 'Find Unused Indexes in PostgreSQL Without Dropping the Wrong One';
const description = 'Tested on PostgreSQL 18 with a streaming replica: an index the primary reported as never scanned served every reporting query on the replica. How to find unused and duplicate indexes safely, and what dropping one saved.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How do I find unused indexes in PostgreSQL?',
    a: 'Query pg_stat_user_indexes for indexes with idx_scan = 0, exclude primary keys, unique and exclusion indexes and any index that backs a constraint, and check last_idx_scan on PostgreSQL 16 and later. Then repeat the query on every read replica, because each server keeps its own counters, and confirm the counters cover a full business cycle.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Does idx_scan include scans on read replicas?',
    a: 'No. Cumulative statistics are per server. A scan on a streaming replica increments idx_scan on that replica only. In a tested PostgreSQL 18 setup, an index used 200 times on the replica showed idx_scan = 0 on the primary.'
  },
  {
    q: 'When are PostgreSQL index usage statistics reset?',
    a: 'On pg_stat_reset() and related reset functions, and after an unclean shutdown: a crash, an immediate shutdown, a start from a base backup or point-in-time recovery. pg_upgrade does not carry cumulative statistics over. A clean restart keeps them. After an immediate shutdown in a test, idx_scan went from 500 to 0 while stats_reset stayed null.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Is it safe to drop an index with idx_scan = 0?',
    a: 'Not by itself. A unique index still enforces uniqueness, an expression index can supply planner statistics, and a rarely used index may serve a monthly job or a replica. Save the definition with pg_get_indexdef, drop it with DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY, and have the CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY statement ready as the rollback.'
  },
  {
    q: 'What does an unused index cost?',
    a: 'Disk, backup and cache space, plus work on every insert and on every update that changes an indexed column. An index on a frequently updated column also prevents HOT updates. In a test, dropping one such index raised HOT updates from 0% to 100% and cut WAL per update from about 14 KB to 4.9 KB.'
  }
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  eyebrow="PostgreSQL indexing · Lab-tested"
  published="2026-11-11"
  updated="2026-11-11"
  schemaType="Article"
  faq={faq}
  ctaTitle="Too many indexes, write latency creeping up, and no one sure what is safe to drop?"
  ctaDescription="An investigation correlates index usage across the primary and every replica with the statements that need each index, then gives you a ranked drop list with rollback statements and the numbers to check afterwards."
>
  <p class="notice"><strong>Short answer:</strong> an index is a drop candidate when <code>pg_stat_user_indexes.idx_scan</code> is 0 <em>on every server that runs queries</em>, the counters cover a full business cycle, and the index does not enforce a constraint. The primary alone is not enough evidence: in the test below, the index that served every reporting query on a replica showed zero scans on the primary.</p>

  <p>Unused indexes are a real cost. Every insert writes to every index, and an update that changes an indexed column writes to all of them and loses the HOT optimization. But the usual one-line query (<code>WHERE idx_scan = 0</code>) is wrong often enough that dropping from it is risky. I built a PostgreSQL 18.6 primary with a streaming replica in Docker, ran a small workload on each, and checked each assumption. The scripts and full output are in the <a href="https://github.com/kiransabne04/databasedeveloper.dev/tree/main/labs/unused-indexes">lab folder</a>.</p>

